# Source: Exercise list "ICT3101 Exercises - Deductive Verification" (HoareLogic Exercises.pdf), problem 5 e), with its solution sheet.
id: sum-up-to-n
title: The sum 0 + 1 + ... + n
topic: Loops
level: 3
source: Exercise list "HoareLogic Exercises", problem 5 e
tags: while, invariant, sum, case analysis

task:
  Give a proof outline and a loop invariant. The postcondition
  $x = 1 + ... + n$ is written $x = sum(k, 0, n, k)$, the sum of $k$ for
  $k$ from $0$ to $n$. The extra term $0$ does not change the value. When
  $n$ is negative the sum has no term, and its value is $0$.

  Hint: fill a table with the values of $x$ and $y$ after each iteration,
  without evaluating the additions.

given:
  Loop condition B: $y < n$

pre: x = 0 && y = 0
post: x = sum(k, 0, n, k)

program:
  {P}
  {I}
  while (y < n) do {
    {I && y < n}
    {R2}
    y := y + 1;      // b1
    {R1}
    x := x + y       // b2
    {I}
  }
  {I && !(y < n)}
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: The invariant I
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  The values at the loop head, without evaluating the additions:

  | Iteration | $y$ | $x$ |
  |---|---|---|
  | 0 | $0$ | $0$ |
  | 1 | $1$ | $0 + 1$ |
  | 2 | $2$ | $0 + 1 + 2$ |
  | 3 | $3$ | $0 + 1 + 2 + 3$ |

  So $x = 0 + 1 + ... + y$, that is $x = sum(k, 0, y, k)$. When $n > 0$,
  $y$ goes from $0$ up to $n$, which gives the bounds $0 <= y <= n$. The
  bound $y <= n$ is needed at the exit, and a lower bound is needed in
  preservation, where the last term of the sum is split off. When
  $n <= 0$ the loop does not run, and $x$ and $y$ stay $0$. The invariant
  has one part for each case.
set: I = (n > 0 ==> 0 <= y <= n && x = sum(k, 0, y, k)) && (n <= 0 ==> x = 0 && y = 0)
text:
  The loop rule with consequence has three premises, with the loop
  condition $B$ being $y < n$: initialisation $P ==> I$, preservation of
  $I$ by the body, and conclusion $I && !B ==> Q$.

step: P implies I
part: Initialisation
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: P, I
text:
  The invariant must hold when the loop is reached. No statement comes
  before the loop, so the precondition $P$ must imply $I$.
  >>> «P» ==> «I»
vc: x = 0 && y = 0 ==> I
why:
  Case $n > 0$: with $y = 0$, $0 <= 0 <= n$ holds, and
  $sum(k, 0, 0, k) = 0 = x$. Case $n <= 0$: $x = 0 && y = 0$ is $P$
  itself.

step: R1, the weakest precondition of x := x + y for I
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b2
text:
  One iteration must keep the invariant:
  >>> {«I» && «y < n : B»} y := y + 1; x := x + y {«I»}
  We calculate the weakest precondition of the body for $I$, from the last
  statement. We substitute $x$ with $x + y$ in $I$.
calc:
  wp(x := x + y, I)
  = ((n > 0 ==> 0 <= y <= n && x = sum(k, 0, y, k)) && (n <= 0 ==> x = 0 && y = 0))[x <- x + y]    // I
  = (n > 0 ==> 0 <= y <= n && x + y = sum(k, 0, y, k)) && (n <= 0 ==> x + y = 0 && y = 0)    // x is replaced by x + y
set: R1 = (n > 0 ==> 0 <= y <= n && x + y = sum(k, 0, y, k)) && (n <= 0 ==> x + y = 0 && y = 0)

step: R2, the weakest precondition of y := y + 1 for R1
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b1
text:
  We substitute $y$ with $y + 1$ in $R1$, computed in the previous step.
calc:
  wp(y := y + 1, R1)
  = ((n > 0 ==> 0 <= y <= n && x + y = sum(k, 0, y, k)) && (n <= 0 ==> x + y = 0 && y = 0))[y <- y + 1]    // R1
  = (n > 0 ==> 0 <= y + 1 <= n && x + (y + 1) = sum(k, 0, y + 1, k)) && (n <= 0 ==> x + (y + 1) = 0 && y + 1 = 0)    // y is replaced by y + 1
  = (n > 0 ==> -1 <= y <= n - 1 && x + (y + 1) = sum(k, 0, y + 1, k)) && (n <= 0 ==> x + (y + 1) = 0 && y + 1 = 0)    // subtract 1 from each part of the bounds
  = (n > 0 ==> -1 <= y <= n - 1 && x = sum(k, 0, y, k)) && (n <= 0 ==> x + (y + 1) = 0 && y + 1 = 0)    // since y >= -1, sum(k, 0, y + 1, k) is sum(k, 0, y, k) + (y + 1); subtract y + 1 from both sides
  = (n > 0 ==> -1 <= y <= n - 1 && x = sum(k, 0, y, k)) && (n <= 0 ==> x = 0 && y = -1)    // y + 1 = 0 is y = -1, and then x + (y + 1) = 0 is x = 0
set: R2 = (n > 0 ==> -1 <= y <= n - 1 && x = sum(k, 0, y, k)) && (n <= 0 ==> x = 0 && y = -1)

step: I and B imply R2
part: Preservation
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: I && y < n, R2
text:
  By the consequence rule, the triple holds when $I && B$ implies $R2$.
  >>> «I» && «y < n : B» ==> «R2»
  We check the two cases of $n$.
vc: I && y < n ==> R2
why:
  Case $n > 0$: $I$ gives $0 <= y$, so $-1 <= y$. For integers, $y < n$
  is $y <= n - 1$. The equation $x = sum(k, 0, y, k)$ is part of $I$.

  Case $n <= 0$: $I$ gives $y = 0$, and $B$ gives $y < n <= 0$. These
  contradict each other, so no state is in this case and the implication
  holds.

step: I and not B imply Q
part: Conclusion
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: I && !(y < n), Q
text:
  When the loop stops, $I$ holds and $B$ is false. This must imply the
  postcondition $Q$.
  >>> «I» && «!(y < n) : ¬B» ==> «Q»
vc: I && !(y < n) ==> x = sum(k, 0, n, k)
why:
  Case $n > 0$: $!(y < n)$ is $y >= n$, and with $y <= n$ it gives
  $y = n$. Then $x = sum(k, 0, y, k)$ becomes $x = sum(k, 0, n, k)$.

  Case $n <= 0$: $I$ gives $x = 0$, and $sum(k, 0, n, k) = 0$: for $n = 0$
  the only term is $0$, and for $n < 0$ there is no term.
result: The triple is valid.

summary:
  The loop also terminates: $n - y$ decreases by one in every iteration,
  and it is positive whenever the body runs, since then $y < n$. So
  $n - y$ is a variant.
